DHom -- D-homomorphisms between holonomic D-modules
***************************************************

  * Usage: 
        DHom(M,N), DHom(M,N,w), DHom(I,J)
  * Inputs:
      * M, a *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, over the Weyl algebra D
      * N, a *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, over the Weyl algebra D
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, which represents the module M
        = D/I
      * J, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, which represents the module N
        = D/J
      * w, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a positive weight vector
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Strategy: DHom_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmStrategy=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value Schreyer,
  * Outputs:
      * a *note hash table: (Macaulay2Doc)HashTable,,  a basis of
        D-homomorphisms between holonomic D-modules M and N

Description
===========

The set of D-homomorphisms between two holonomic modules M and N is a
finite-dimensional vector space over the ground field. Since a homomorphism is
defined by where it sends a set of generators, the output of this command is a
list of matrices whose columns correspond to the images of the generators of M.
Here the generators of M are determined from its presentation by generators and
relations.

The procedure calls *note Drestriction: Drestriction,, which uses w if
specified.

The algorithm used appears in the paper 'Computing homomorphisms between
holonomic D-modules' by Tsai-Walther(2000). The method is to combine
isomorphisms of Bjork and Kashiwara with the restriction algorithm.

+-----------------------------------------------+
|i1 : W = QQ[x, D, WeylAlgebra=>{x=>D}]         |
|                                               |
|o1 = W                                         |
|                                               |
|o1 : PolynomialRing, 1 differential variable(s)|
+-----------------------------------------------+
|i2 : M = W^1/ideal(D-1)                        |
|                                               |
|o2 = cokernel | D-1 |                          |
|                                               |
|                            1                  |
|o2 : W-module, quotient of W                   |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|i3 : N = W^1/ideal((D-1)^2)                    |
|                                               |
|o3 = cokernel | D2-2D+1 |                      |
|                                               |
|                            1                  |
|o3 : W-module, quotient of W                   |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|i4 : DHom(M,N)                                 |
|                                               |
|o4 = {| -xD+x+1 |, | -D+1 |}                   |
|                                               |
|o4 : List                                      |
+-----------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

Input modules M, N, D/I and D/J should be holonomic.

See also
========

  * *note DExt: DExt, -- Ext groups between holonomic modules
  * *note Drestriction: Drestriction, -- restriction modules of a D-module

Ways to use DHom:
=================

  * "DHom(Ideal,Ideal)"
  * "DHom(Module,Module)"
  * "DHom(Module,Module,List)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note DHom: DHom, is a *note method function with options:
(Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunctionWithOptions,.

