File: BernsteinSato.info, Node: Dlocalize, Next: Dlocalize_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmStrategy=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, Prev: DintegrationIdeal_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmStrategy=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, Up: Top

Dlocalize -- localization of a D-module
***************************************

  * Usage: 
        Dlocalize(M,f), Dlocalize(I,f)
  * Inputs:
      * M, a *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, over the Weyl algebra D
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, which represents the module M
        = D/I
      * f, a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, a polynomial
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Strategy: Dlocalize_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmStrategy=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value OTW, strategy for computing a localization of a D-module
  * Outputs:
      * a *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, the localized module M_f =
        M[f^{-1}] as a D-module

Description
===========

One of the nice things about D-modules is that if a finitely generated D-module
is specializable along f, then it's localization with respect to f is also
finitely generated.  For instance, this is true for all holonomic D-modules.


There are two different algorithms for localization implemented. The first
appears in the paper 'A localization algorithm for D-modules' by
Oaku-Takayama-Walther (1999).  The second is due to Oaku and appears in the
paper 'Algorithmic computation of local cohomology modules and the
cohomological dimension of algebraic varieties' by Walther(1999)
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : W = QQ[x,y,Dx,Dy, WeylAlgebra => {x=>Dx,y=>Dy}]|
|                                                    |
|o1 = W                                              |
|                                                    |
|o1 : PolynomialRing, 2 differential variable(s)     |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : M = W^1/(ideal(x*Dx+1, Dy))                    |
|                                                    |
|o2 = cokernel | xDx+1 Dy |                          |
|                                                    |
|                            1                       |
|o2 : W-module, quotient of W                        |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : f = x^2-y^3                                    |
|                                                    |
|        3    2                                      |
|o3 = - y  + x                                       |
|                                                    |
|o3 : W                                              |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : Mf = Dlocalize(M, f)                           |
|                                                    |
|o4 = cokernel | -3xDx-2yDy-15 -y3Dy+x2Dy-6y2 |      |
|                                                    |
|                            1                       |
|o4 : W-module, quotient of W                        |
+----------------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note DlocalizeAll: DlocalizeAll, -- localization of a D-module (extended
    version)
  * *note DlocalizeMap: DlocalizeMap, -- localization map from a D-module to
    its localization
  * *note AnnFs: AnnFs, -- differential annihilator of a polynomial in a Weyl
    algebra
  * *note Dintegration: Dintegration, -- integration modules of a D-module

Ways to use Dlocalize:
======================

  * "Dlocalize(Ideal,RingElement)"
  * "Dlocalize(Module,RingElement)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note Dlocalize: Dlocalize, is a *note method function with options:
(Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunctionWithOptions,.

