Drestriction -- restriction modules of a D-module
*************************************************

  * Usage: 
        N = Drestriction(M,w), NI = Drestriction(I,w), Ni = Drestriction(i,M,w),
        NIi = Drestriction(i,I,w)
  * Inputs:
      * M, a *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, over the Weyl algebra D
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, which represents the module M
        = D/I
      * w, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a weight vector
      * i, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, nonnegative
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Strategy: DintegrationIdeal_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmStrategy=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,
        => ..., default value Schreyer,
  * Outputs:
      * Ni, a *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, the i-th derived integration
        module of M with respect to the weight vector w
      * N, a *note hash table: (Macaulay2Doc)HashTable,, contains entries of
        the form i=>Ni
      * NIi, a *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, the i-th derived
        integration module of D/I with respect to the weight vector w
      * NI, a *note hash table: (Macaulay2Doc)HashTable,, contains entries of
        the form i=>NIi

Description
===========

The derived restriction modules of a D-module M are the derived inverse images
in the sense of algebraic geometry but in the category of D-modules. This
routine computes restrictions to coordinate subspaces, where the subspace is
determined by the strictly positive entries of the weight vectorw, e.g., {x_i =
0 : w_i > 0} if D = C<x_1,...,x_n,d_1,...,d_n>. The input weight vector should
be a list of n numbers to induce the weight (-w,w) on D.

The algorithm used appears in the paper 'Algorithms for D-modules' by
Oaku-Takayama(1999).  The method is to compute an adapted resolution with
respect to the weight vector w and use the b-function with respect to w to
truncate the resolution.

+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[x_1,x_2,D_1,D_2,WeylAlgebra=>{x_1=>D_1,x_2=>D_2}]|
|                                                             |
|o1 = R                                                       |
|                                                             |
|o1 : PolynomialRing, 2 differential variable(s)              |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : I = ideal(x_1, D_2-1)                                   |
|                                                             |
|o2 = ideal (x , D  - 1)                                      |
|             1   2                                           |
|                                                             |
|o2 : Ideal of R                                              |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : Drestriction(I,{1,0})                                   |
|                                                             |
|o3 = HashTable{0 => 0                 }                      |
|               1 => cokernel | D_2-1 |                       |
|                                                             |
|o3 : HashTable                                               |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

The module M should be specializable to the subspace. This is true for
holonomic modules.The weight vector w should be a list of n numbers if M is a
module over the n-th Weyl algebra.

See also
========

  * *note DrestrictionAll: DrestrictionAll, -- restriction modules of a
